WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.083
Welcome to Week Eleven of CS104.

2
00:00:03.083 --> 00:00:09.250
This lecture studies recursion: defining a new value in terms of values

3
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.278
already known.

4
00:00:10.278 --> 00:00:15.931
The first part constructs sequences from initial conditions and recurrence rules.

5
00:00:15.931 --> 00:00:22.097
We will see Fibonacci numbers in a rabbit model, count the moves

6
00:00:22.097 --> 00:00:28.264
in the Tower of Hanoi, and reason about ways to climb stairs.

7
00:00:28.264 --> 00:00:34.431
The second part asks for explicit formulas, which give the value at

8
00:00:34.431 --> 00:00:38.028
an index without calculating every earlier term.

9
00:00:38.028 --> 00:00:44.194
We will derive a characteristic equation for second-order recurrences, then work through

10
00:00:44.194 --> 00:00:47.792
the cases of distinct and repeated roots.

11
00:00:47.792 --> 00:00:53.958
The slide calls this Lecture Nine, while this course portal places it

12
00:00:53.958 --> 00:00:55.500
in Week Eleven.

13
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.698
Here is the route.

14
00:00:57.698 --> 00:01:01.545
Section nine point one defines sequences recursively.

15
00:01:01.545 --> 00:01:08.140
An initial condition anchors the sequence, and a recurrence tells us how

16
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.788
to continue it.

17
00:01:09.788 --> 00:01:16.383
Arithmetic and geometric sequences show simple one-step rules; Fibonacci needs two earlier

18
00:01:16.383 --> 00:01:16.932
terms.

19
00:01:16.932 --> 00:01:22.977
The rabbit and Hanoi stories turn those rules into counting models.

20
00:01:22.977 --> 00:01:29.572
Section nine point two narrows to second-order linear homogeneous recurrences with constant

21
00:01:29.572 --> 00:01:30.122
coefficients.

22
00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:36.716
We will try solutions that are powers of a number, solve a

23
00:01:36.716 --> 00:01:43.311
quadratic characteristic equation, and use initial values to select one particular sequence.

24
00:01:43.311 --> 00:01:49.356
The two algebraic cases are distinct roots and a repeated root.

25
00:01:49.356 --> 00:01:55.950
Keep the difference between a rule and an explicit formula in mind

26
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.500
throughout.

27
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.168
We begin by defining a sequence recursively.

28
00:02:00.168 --> 00:02:06.457
A sequence is an ordered collection of values indexed by integers, often

29
00:02:06.457 --> 00:02:08.029
starting at zero.

30
00:02:08.029 --> 00:02:14.317
A recursive description has two ingredients: enough starting values, and a rule

31
00:02:14.317 --> 00:02:18.510
that expresses each later value using earlier ones.

32
00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:24.798
The starting values matter because a rule such as take the previous

33
00:02:24.798 --> 00:02:31.087
term and add three describes many different sequences until we say where

34
00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:32.135
it begins.

35
00:02:32.135 --> 00:02:38.423
If the rule looks back two positions, we usually need two initial

36
00:02:38.423 --> 00:02:38.947
values.

37
00:02:38.947 --> 00:02:45.236
This week builds on the multiple-base-case reasoning from the induction lecture: the

38
00:02:45.236 --> 00:02:51.000
number of starting facts should match the dependencies in the recurrence.

39
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.198
The slide gives three familiar recursive definitions.

40
00:02:54.198 --> 00:02:59.681
For an arithmetic sequence, start with a sub zero equal to c,

41
00:02:59.681 --> 00:03:05.164
then add the fixed difference d each time: a sub n equals

42
00:03:05.164 --> 00:03:08.362
a sub n minus one plus d.

43
00:03:08.362 --> 00:03:12.017
Its explicit form is c plus n d.

44
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:17.500
For a geometric sequence, start at c and multiply by the fixed

45
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:22.526
ratio r each time, giving c times r to the n.

46
00:03:22.526 --> 00:03:28.009
Factorial starts with zero factorial equal to one and multiplies the preceding

47
00:03:28.009 --> 00:03:31.664
factorial by n for n at least one.

48
00:03:31.664 --> 00:03:36.690
For example, four factorial is four times three factorial, or twenty-four.

49
00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:42.172
Notice that each rule needs its initial condition; the recurrence alone does

50
00:03:42.172 --> 00:03:44.000
not determine the values.

51
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.583
Definition nine point one point one introduces the Fibonacci sequence used in

52
00:03:49.583 --> 00:03:50.513
this deck.

53
00:03:50.513 --> 00:03:56.096
Its first two values are f sub zero equals one and f

54
00:03:56.096 --> 00:03:57.957
sub one equals one.

55
00:03:57.957 --> 00:04:03.539
From index two onward, each term is the sum of the previous

56
00:04:03.539 --> 00:04:09.122
two: f sub n equals f sub n minus one plus f

57
00:04:09.122 --> 00:04:10.983
sub n minus two.

58
00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:16.565
So the sequence begins one, one, two, three, five, eight, and thirteen.

59
00:04:16.565 --> 00:04:22.148
Some books use zero and one as their first two values, which

60
00:04:22.148 --> 00:04:23.543
shifts the indices.

61
00:04:23.543 --> 00:04:27.730
In this lesson we keep the slide's one-and-one convention.

62
00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:33.313
The spiral images illustrate a common visual association, but the mathematical definition

63
00:04:33.313 --> 00:04:37.500
is the recurrence together with those two initial values.

64
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.881
The rabbit story explains why a two-term recurrence appears.

65
00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:46.748
One pair is born at the beginning of the year.

66
00:04:46.748 --> 00:04:52.588
A pair does not reproduce during its first month, then produces one

67
00:04:52.588 --> 00:04:58.429
new pair at the end of each later month; no rabbits die.

68
00:04:58.429 --> 00:05:04.270
To count accurately, track pairs rather than individual animals, because every birth

69
00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.730
produces one pair.

70
00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:11.571
At the end of a month, the population consists of all pairs

71
00:05:11.571 --> 00:05:15.465
alive one month earlier plus the newborn pairs.

72
00:05:15.465 --> 00:05:21.305
The newborns come from pairs old enough to reproduce, which correspond to

73
00:05:21.305 --> 00:05:24.226
the pairs counted two months earlier.

74
00:05:24.226 --> 00:05:30.066
This separation between existing and newly born pairs leads to the Fibonacci

75
00:05:30.066 --> 00:05:32.500
rule on the next slide.

76
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:37.344
Let r sub n count rabbit pairs alive at the end of

77
00:05:37.344 --> 00:05:41.785
month n, and let a sub n count pairs born then.

78
00:05:41.785 --> 00:05:46.630
No pair dies, so r sub n equals r sub n minus

79
00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:48.648
one plus a sub n.

80
00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:53.493
The reproductive timing gives a sub n equal to r sub n

81
00:05:53.493 --> 00:05:54.300
minus two.

82
00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:59.144
Therefore r sub n equals r sub n minus one plus r

83
00:05:59.144 --> 00:06:03.989
sub n minus two for n at least two, with r sub

84
00:06:03.989 --> 00:06:07.622
zero and r sub one both equal to one.

85
00:06:07.622 --> 00:06:10.448
The values match this deck's Fibonacci sequence.

86
00:06:10.448 --> 00:06:15.293
At the end of twelve months there are r sub twelve, or

87
00:06:15.293 --> 00:06:20.137
two hundred thirty-three pairs, which means four hundred sixty-six individual rabbits under

88
00:06:20.137 --> 00:06:20.944
the model.

89
00:06:20.944 --> 00:06:25.789
State the unit when reporting the answer: pairs and rabbits are not

90
00:06:25.789 --> 00:06:27.000
the same count.

91
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.703
Now consider the Tower of Hanoi.

92
00:06:29.703 --> 00:06:35.108
All k disks begin on one pole, ordered with the largest at

93
00:06:35.108 --> 00:06:36.009
the bottom.

94
00:06:36.009 --> 00:06:41.414
We want to transfer the stack to another pole using a third

95
00:06:41.414 --> 00:06:41.865
pole.

96
00:06:41.865 --> 00:06:47.270
A move carries exactly one disk, and a larger disk may never

97
00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.523
sit on a smaller one.

98
00:06:49.523 --> 00:06:54.928
Counting random legal moves is not the goal; we want the minimum

99
00:06:54.928 --> 00:06:55.829
number needed.

100
00:06:55.829 --> 00:07:01.234
The largest disk cannot move until every smaller disk is out of

101
00:07:01.234 --> 00:07:02.135
its way.

102
00:07:02.135 --> 00:07:07.541
That observation suggests a recursive plan: move the smaller tower aside, move

103
00:07:07.541 --> 00:07:12.495
the largest disk once, then move the smaller tower onto it.

104
00:07:12.495 --> 00:07:17.000
The next two slides turn that plan into a recurrence.

105
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.641
Read the diagram as three stages.

106
00:07:19.641 --> 00:07:24.923
First, transfer the top k minus one disks from pole A to

107
00:07:24.923 --> 00:07:26.684
the spare pole B.

108
00:07:26.684 --> 00:07:30.645
The largest base disk is now exposed on A.

109
00:07:30.645 --> 00:07:35.927
Second, move that one base disk from A to the destination pole

110
00:07:35.927 --> 00:07:36.368
C.

111
00:07:36.368 --> 00:07:41.209
Third, transfer the k minus one disks from B onto C.

112
00:07:41.209 --> 00:07:46.491
Each smaller-tower transfer is itself the same kind of Hanoi problem, just

113
00:07:46.491 --> 00:07:48.252
with one fewer disk.

114
00:07:48.252 --> 00:07:53.534
The rules force these stages if we want to move the largest

115
00:07:53.534 --> 00:07:56.615
disk and finish with a complete stack.

116
00:07:56.615 --> 00:08:01.897
Thus the diagram is both a constructive strategy and a way to

117
00:08:01.897 --> 00:08:07.179
see why any solution needs at least the two smaller transfers and

118
00:08:07.179 --> 00:08:08.500
one base-disk move.

119
00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:13.717
Let m sub k be the minimum number of moves for k

120
00:08:13.717 --> 00:08:14.152
disks.

121
00:08:14.152 --> 00:08:18.935
The first smaller-tower transfer costs m sub k minus one moves.

122
00:08:18.935 --> 00:08:21.543
The base disk costs one move.

123
00:08:21.543 --> 00:08:26.761
The final smaller-tower transfer costs another m sub k minus one moves.

124
00:08:26.761 --> 00:08:31.978
Therefore m sub k equals two times m sub k minus one

125
00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:35.022
plus one for k at least two.

126
00:08:35.022 --> 00:08:38.935
With just one disk, m sub one equals one.

127
00:08:38.935 --> 00:08:44.152
The recursive strategy achieves this count, and the largest disk cannot be

128
00:08:44.152 --> 00:08:48.935
moved without clearing the smaller disks, so it is a minimum.

129
00:08:48.935 --> 00:08:54.152
The extra plus one is easy to overlook: it is the single

130
00:08:54.152 --> 00:08:58.500
move of the largest disk between the two recursive calls.

131
00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.739
Unroll the Hanoi recurrence for the first few cases.

132
00:09:02.739 --> 00:09:08.391
One disk needs one move; two need three; three need seven; four

133
00:09:08.391 --> 00:09:09.333
need fifteen.

134
00:09:09.333 --> 00:09:13.572
These are one less than successive powers of two.

135
00:09:13.572 --> 00:09:19.225
The slide writes the pattern as one plus two plus four and

136
00:09:19.225 --> 00:09:24.406
so on through two raised to the exponent k minus one.

137
00:09:24.406 --> 00:09:30.058
That geometric sum is two raised to exponent k, then subtract one.

138
00:09:30.058 --> 00:09:35.710
We can verify it by substitution: if m at index k minus

139
00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:41.362
one is one less than two raised to exponent k minus one,

140
00:09:41.362 --> 00:09:47.014
then twice that value plus one is one less than two raised

141
00:09:47.014 --> 00:09:48.428
to exponent k.

142
00:09:48.428 --> 00:09:54.080
For thirty disks the minimum is two raised to the thirtieth power,

143
00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.732
then subtract one: one billion seventy-three million seven hundred forty-one thousand eight

144
00:09:59.732 --> 00:10:01.145
hundred twenty-three moves.

145
00:10:01.145 --> 00:10:03.500
Recursive growth becomes enormous quickly.

146
00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:08.353
The stair-climbing problem asks for the number of ways to reach the

147
00:10:08.353 --> 00:10:11.993
top when every move climbs one or two stairs.

148
00:10:11.993 --> 00:10:16.846
Let s sub n be the number of ways to climb n

149
00:10:16.846 --> 00:10:17.250
stairs.

150
00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:20.081
Classify a route by its last move.

151
00:10:20.081 --> 00:10:24.934
If that move is one stair, everything before it is a way

152
00:10:24.934 --> 00:10:27.360
to climb n minus one stairs.

153
00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.213
If it is two, everything before it is a way to climb

154
00:10:32.213 --> 00:10:33.426
n minus two.

155
00:10:33.426 --> 00:10:38.279
The two classes do not overlap, so s sub n equals s

156
00:10:38.279 --> 00:10:42.324
sub n minus one plus s sub n minus two.

157
00:10:42.324 --> 00:10:47.176
There is one empty route for zero stairs and one route for

158
00:10:47.176 --> 00:10:52.029
one stair, giving s sub zero equals s sub one equals one.

159
00:10:52.029 --> 00:10:56.882
Thus the counts match this lecture's Fibonacci indexing: one, one, two, three,

160
00:10:56.882 --> 00:10:58.500
five, and so on.

161
00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.238
Section nine point two changes the question.

162
00:11:02.238 --> 00:11:08.646
A recurrence tells us how to compute the next term from earlier

163
00:11:08.646 --> 00:11:15.053
terms, but an explicit formula gives a term directly from its index.

164
00:11:15.053 --> 00:11:21.461
We focus on a specific family: second-order linear homogeneous recurrence relations with

165
00:11:21.461 --> 00:11:22.529
constant coefficients.

166
00:11:22.529 --> 00:11:26.801
Second-order means the rule reaches back two terms.

167
00:11:26.801 --> 00:11:33.209
Linear means each earlier term appears only to the first power, with

168
00:11:33.209 --> 00:11:35.879
no products of sequence terms.

169
00:11:35.879 --> 00:11:40.150
Homogeneous means there is no independent added term.

170
00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:46.024
Constant coefficients mean the multipliers do not vary with the index.

171
00:11:46.024 --> 00:11:52.432
For this family, a quadratic equation provides a practical route to an

172
00:11:52.432 --> 00:11:53.500
explicit expression.

173
00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:58.900
The precise form is a sub k equals A times a sub

174
00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:04.300
k minus one plus B times a sub k minus two, for

175
00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:09.700
k at least two, where A and B are fixed real numbers

176
00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:11.500
and B is nonzero.

177
00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.300
The slide sorts examples.

178
00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:18.700
Three times the previous term plus two times the term before fits.

179
00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:24.100
So does a rule using only the term two steps back: take

180
00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:25.900
A equal to zero.

181
00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:29.500
A rule reaching back three terms is third-order.

182
00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:34.450
A square or product of sequence terms makes a rule nonlinear.

183
00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:37.600
An extra plus one makes it nonhomogeneous.

184
00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.100
A multiplier such as k minus one makes coefficients variable.

185
00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:47.500
Classification matters because the characteristic-root formulas ahead apply to this exact family.

186
00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:52.318
To derive the characteristic equation, try a sequence of powers: a sub

187
00:12:52.318 --> 00:12:55.932
k equals t to the k, with t nonzero.

188
00:12:55.932 --> 00:13:00.750
Substitute this guess into a sub k equals A a sub k

189
00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:04.364
minus one plus B a sub k minus two.

190
00:13:04.364 --> 00:13:09.182
We obtain t to the k equals A times t to the

191
00:13:09.182 --> 00:13:14.000
k minus one plus B times t to the k minus two.

192
00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.614
Divide through by t to the k minus two.

193
00:13:17.614 --> 00:13:22.432
The result is t squared minus A t minus B equals zero.

194
00:13:22.432 --> 00:13:27.250
Every root t of that quadratic produces a power sequence satisfying the

195
00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:27.652
recurrence.

196
00:13:27.652 --> 00:13:32.470
Be careful with the sign on B: the recurrence has plus B

197
00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:37.288
on the right, so the characteristic polynomial has minus B on the

198
00:13:37.288 --> 00:13:37.689
left.

199
00:13:37.689 --> 00:13:40.500
Initial values have not been used yet.

200
00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:45.573
For a concrete example, the recurrence is a sub k equals a

201
00:13:45.573 --> 00:13:50.224
sub k minus one plus two a sub k minus two.

202
00:13:50.224 --> 00:13:55.297
Here A is one and B is two, so the characteristic equation

203
00:13:55.297 --> 00:13:59.102
is t squared minus t minus two equals zero.

204
00:13:59.102 --> 00:14:03.329
Factor it as t minus two times t plus one.

205
00:14:03.329 --> 00:14:08.402
The distinct roots are two and negative one, giving the power sequences

206
00:14:08.402 --> 00:14:12.630
two to the k and negative one to the k.

207
00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:14.321
Each solves the recurrence.

208
00:14:14.321 --> 00:14:19.394
Because the rule is linear and homogeneous, adding scaled solutions also gives

209
00:14:19.394 --> 00:14:20.240
a solution.

210
00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:25.313
Thus a general candidate is C times two to the k plus

211
00:14:25.313 --> 00:14:28.272
D times negative one to the k.

212
00:14:28.272 --> 00:14:32.500
The constants depend on whatever first two terms we specify.

213
00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:35.750
The distinct-roots theorem makes that candidate general.

214
00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:41.321
If the characteristic equation has two different roots r and s, every

215
00:14:41.321 --> 00:14:46.893
sequence satisfying this second-order recurrence has the form a sub k equals

216
00:14:46.893 --> 00:14:52.000
C r to the k plus D s to the k.

217
00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:55.250
The first two values determine the constants.

218
00:14:55.250 --> 00:15:00.357
At k equals zero, a sub zero equals C plus D.

219
00:15:00.357 --> 00:15:05.929
At k equals one, a sub one equals C r plus D

220
00:15:05.929 --> 00:15:06.393
s.

221
00:15:06.393 --> 00:15:11.964
Because r and s differ, these two equations have a unique solution

222
00:15:11.964 --> 00:15:13.821
for C and D.

223
00:15:13.821 --> 00:15:19.393
The slide omits the full theorem proof, but the structure is useful:

224
00:15:19.393 --> 00:15:24.500
roots determine the building-block sequences, while initial conditions select their combination.

225
00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:29.854
Apply the distinct-root method to this deck's Fibonacci sequence, with f sub

226
00:15:29.854 --> 00:15:33.869
zero and f sub one both equal to one.

227
00:15:33.869 --> 00:15:39.223
The recurrence has A and B both equal to one, so its

228
00:15:39.223 --> 00:15:44.131
characteristic equation is t squared minus t minus one equals zero.

229
00:15:44.131 --> 00:15:46.808
The quadratic formula gives two roots.

230
00:15:46.808 --> 00:15:52.162
Phi equals the quantity one plus square root of five, all over

231
00:15:52.162 --> 00:15:52.608
two.

232
00:15:52.608 --> 00:15:57.962
Psi equals the quantity one minus square root of five, all over

233
00:15:57.962 --> 00:15:58.408
two.

234
00:15:58.408 --> 00:16:03.762
We therefore write f sub k as C times phi to the

235
00:16:03.762 --> 00:16:08.223
power k plus D times psi to the power k.

236
00:16:08.223 --> 00:16:13.577
The next step is not another recurrence calculation; it is solving for

237
00:16:13.577 --> 00:16:17.146
C and D from the two starting values.

238
00:16:17.146 --> 00:16:22.500
Remember that this convention is shifted from the common zero-one Fibonacci indexing.

239
00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:27.425
Use f sub zero equals one to get C plus D equals

240
00:16:27.425 --> 00:16:27.836
one.

241
00:16:27.836 --> 00:16:32.761
Use f sub one equals one to get C phi plus D

242
00:16:32.761 --> 00:16:33.993
psi equals one.

243
00:16:33.993 --> 00:16:38.918
Solving gives C equal to phi divided by square root of five

244
00:16:38.918 --> 00:16:43.843
and D equal to negative psi divided by square root of five.

245
00:16:43.843 --> 00:16:48.769
Substituting yields f sub k equal to phi raised to exponent k

246
00:16:48.769 --> 00:16:53.694
plus one, minus psi raised to exponent k plus one, with the

247
00:16:53.694 --> 00:16:56.978
whole difference divided by square root of five.

248
00:16:56.978 --> 00:17:01.903
Check the index: at k equals zero, the numerator is phi minus

249
00:17:01.903 --> 00:17:06.828
psi, which is square root of five, so the formula gives one.

250
00:17:06.828 --> 00:17:10.112
At k equals one it also gives one.

251
00:17:10.112 --> 00:17:15.037
The exponent k plus one reflects this deck's f sub zero equals

252
00:17:15.037 --> 00:17:17.500
f sub one equals one convention.

253
00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:22.573
What if the characteristic quadratic has only one real root because the

254
00:17:22.573 --> 00:17:24.264
same root occurs twice?

255
00:17:24.264 --> 00:17:29.336
We cannot use two copies of r to the k as independent

256
00:17:29.336 --> 00:17:30.182
building blocks.

257
00:17:30.182 --> 00:17:35.255
The single-root theorem supplies a second form, k times r to the

258
00:17:35.255 --> 00:17:35.677
k.

259
00:17:35.677 --> 00:17:40.750
The general solution is a sub k equals C r to the

260
00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:45.823
k plus D k r to the k, or equivalently C plus

261
00:17:45.823 --> 00:17:48.782
D k times r to the k.

262
00:17:48.782 --> 00:17:51.741
Again two known values determine the constants.

263
00:17:51.741 --> 00:17:56.814
The zero-index value is especially convenient: at k equals zero, the D

264
00:17:56.814 --> 00:18:00.195
term vanishes, so C equals a sub zero.

265
00:18:00.195 --> 00:18:04.000
The next example uses a repeated root of two.

266
00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:08.828
The example defines b sub k as four times b sub k

267
00:18:08.828 --> 00:18:13.656
minus one minus four times b sub k minus two, beginning with

268
00:18:13.656 --> 00:18:18.082
b sub zero equals one and b sub one equals three.

269
00:18:18.082 --> 00:18:21.703
Here A is four and B is negative four.

270
00:18:21.703 --> 00:18:26.531
The characteristic polynomial is t squared minus four t plus four, which

271
00:18:26.531 --> 00:18:29.750
factors as the square of t minus two.

272
00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.969
There is a repeated root r equals two.

273
00:18:32.969 --> 00:18:37.797
Therefore write b sub k as C times two to the k

274
00:18:37.797 --> 00:18:41.418
plus D times k times two to the k.

275
00:18:41.418 --> 00:18:46.246
We cannot determine C and D from the recurrence alone; the two

276
00:18:46.246 --> 00:18:48.660
given starting values will fix them.

277
00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:53.488
Notice that the coefficient B is allowed to be negative, as long

278
00:18:53.488 --> 00:18:55.500
as it is not zero.

279
00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:58.193
Now substitute the two starting values.

280
00:18:58.193 --> 00:19:03.579
At k equals zero, b sub zero equals C, so C is

281
00:19:03.579 --> 00:19:04.028
one.

282
00:19:04.028 --> 00:19:09.413
At k equals one, b sub one equals two C plus two

283
00:19:09.413 --> 00:19:14.799
D, so three equals two plus two D and D is one

284
00:19:14.799 --> 00:19:15.248
half.

285
00:19:15.248 --> 00:19:20.634
The explicit formula is b sub k equals two to the k

286
00:19:20.634 --> 00:19:23.327
times one plus k over two.

287
00:19:23.327 --> 00:19:28.713
Check k equals two: the formula gives four times two, or eight,

288
00:19:28.713 --> 00:19:34.098
and the recurrence gives four times three minus four times one, also

289
00:19:34.098 --> 00:19:34.547
eight.

290
00:19:34.547 --> 00:19:39.933
The highlighted closing question asks what happens when the characteristic equation has

291
00:19:39.933 --> 00:19:41.280
no real root.

292
00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:46.665
That complex-root case is beyond the two real-root formulas developed here; it

293
00:19:46.665 --> 00:19:52.051
is a useful next question, not a reason to apply either theorem

294
00:19:52.051 --> 00:19:52.500
blindly.
